{"headline":"You trained all three muscle groups this week — a solid foundation to build on.","summary":"Good work getting through a full week of training and hitting chest, back, and legs across 12 sets. For a 28-year-old male at a healthy BMI of 23.5 with a muscle-building goal, you have a reasonable base to work from. Volume is on the lower end for hypertrophy — most research points to 10 or more sets per muscle group per week as a productive target — so there is clear room to grow. The tonnage numbers look solid relative to your bodyweight, which is encouraging. The main focus now is building consistency and gradually adding more volume and load over time.","progressiveOverload":{"status":"insufficient_data","notes":"No strength index data was recorded this period, so it is not possible to confirm whether loads are trending upward compared to previous sessions. Without lift-by-lift tracking, progressive overload cannot be verified. Logging specific exercises with weights and reps each session is essential to track whether you are actually getting stronger over time, which is the primary driver of muscle growth."},"muscleImbalances":[{"muscleGroup":"legs","severity":"mild","observation":"Legs account for 29% of volume and sit noticeably below chest and back in both sets and tonnage share. While the gap is not alarming at this stage, legs are a large and metabolically important muscle group for overall muscle building and should be brought closer to parity with the upper body over the coming weeks."}],"deloadRecommended":false,"deloadReason":null,"actionItems":["Start logging specific exercises, weights, and reps every session so that progressive overload can be tracked — this is the single most important habit change you can make right now.","Gradually increase your weekly sets per muscle group toward 10 or more over the next few weeks, adding no more than 1 to 2 sets per muscle group per week to manage fatigue.","Give extra attention to legs by adding at least one additional set per session, aiming to bring leg volume closer to the 33 percent share that chest and back currently enjoy.","Carry forward the current momentum from last period by maintaining or slightly increasing the loads used on your main lifts each session, even if the jump is small."],"contextSummary":"Low but consistent volume across chest, back, and legs with no strength index data available; main priorities for next period are increasing sets per muscle group, closing the leg volume gap, and beginning to log individual lift data to enable overload tracking."}